#!/opt/bin/perl

=head1 NAME

mcp-fs - filesystem and command tools with optional sandboxing

=head1 SYNOPSIS

   mcp-fs --stdio /path/to/workdir
   mcp-fs --name project --bind 127.0.0.1:31741 /path/to/workdir

=head1 DESCRIPTION

C<mcp-fs> gives an MCP client tools to read, search and change files, and
run commands. An assistant can use these tools to work on your projects
without you writing a Perl server.

Start it with one or more project directories, called workdirs. The client
connects to the server, lists its tools and makes requests. A request can
select one of these workdirs; with only one, the selection can be omitted.
Tools operate on files on disk. To work with unsaved Vim buffers, use
L<mcp-vim>.

Before starting an operation, the server checks permission rules and, when
needed, asks you for approval. Operations normally run in a bubblewrap
sandbox with the approved access. Workdirs provide the default filesystem
scope; operations needing access elsewhere require the corresponding
sandbox grants.

You can connect an MCP client directly over HTTP or stdin/stdout, or run
the server through C<mcp-multibroker>. See L<AnyEvent::MCP::Intro> for a
walkthrough from installation to a first filesystem request.

=head1 FIRST USE

Configure your MCP client to launch the server with an existing project:

   mcp-fs --stdio --name project /home/alice/src/project

The client starts the process and speaks MCP over stdin/stdout. Do not run
another copy in a terminal. For HTTP clients, run without C<--stdio> and
use C<--print-url> with the same C<--name> and C<--bind> settings; see
L<AnyEvent::MCP::Intro/YOUR FIRST FILESYSTEM SERVER>.

To inspect the tools available with your installed utilities without
starting a listener, run C<mcp-fs --tool-help /home/alice/src/project>.

Once connected, ask the assistant:

   Show the configured workdirs and list my project's top-level files.
   Read its README if present. Do not change files or run commands.

C<workdirs> shows the available paths without a worker. Listing requests
C<inspect> permission, and reading a file requests C<read>. Allow each once
to try them. Approval is specific to the requested action and sandbox grants.
If no dialog appears, inspect the tool error and server stderr: the helper
needs a YAD display or usable terminal. See L</PERMISSIONS>.

For a separate sandbox check without MCP, change to your project directory
and run:

   mcp-fs --exec-cmd workdir-read -- ls

This uses the current directory as the workdir. It tests the sandbox,
not client connectivity or permission prompting.

=head1 TOOLS

C<workdirs> reports configured workdirs and available sandbox grants.
C<list> and C<find> enumerate directory entries; C<stat> returns lstat
metadata without following symlinks. C<file-read> reads byte ranges and
returns Base64 data; C<read-lines> reads LF-delimited lines with selectable
UTF-8, Latin-1 or Base64 representation. C<grep> searches files using a
literal pattern or PCRE. C<file-write> writes Base64 bytes at an offset
without implicit truncation; C<path-op> performs pathname mutations;
C<file-patch> applies a unified diff to a single file.

C<exec> runs an argv array, not a shell command string. To use shell
syntax, explicitly invoke a shell in that array. C<stdin_encoding> selects
C<utf-8> (default), C<latin1> or C<base64>. The 4 MiB limit applies to
the resulting stdin bytes. Stdout and stderr are decoded as
UTF-8, with a raw capture limit of 1 MiB per stream; use the byte-range tools
for binary file contents. Its optional
C<sandbox_grants> B<replaces> the default C<workdir-read> grant rather than
adding to it. Explicitly passing an empty array therefore asks for no
grants. Command exit codes are normal tool results, not transport errors.
On timeout, output collection stops and C<truncated> is true; C<exit> and
C<signal> still describe the reaped child.
Perl is required for normal server operation; missing optional GNU utilities
can cause their tools to be omitted.
The command requested by C<exec> is resolved when the worker runs; it need
not be present when the server starts. Inspect C<tools/list> rather than
assuming every tool is present.

=head2 Command output

C<exec>, C<vcs-diff> and C<vcs-status> share three presentation options:

=over

=item grep => $pattern

Optional PCRE line filter for stdout. It runs in a separate timed GNU grep
worker after command output has been captured. It is not passed to the
original command or a shell. Stderr remains unfiltered.

=item max_lines => $integer

Maximum returned stdout lines after filtering, default 2000, maximum 100000.

=item max_bytes => $integer

Maximum returned UTF-8 bytes per stream, default 65536, maximum 1048576.
Clipping does not leave an incomplete UTF-8 character. Stderr has its own
budget, so a large stdout does not hide diagnostics.

=back

The command runs once and is drained to completion, subject to its timeout.
Raw capture is bounded to 1 MiB per stream, independently of the returned
output limits. C<capture_truncated> means bytes were omitted before filtering
or collection ended on timeout: matches in that omitted output are unknown.
C<stdout_truncated>, C<stderr_truncated> and the combined C<truncated> flag
also account for returned-output limits.

Changing presentation options does not change the command's permission
subject. C<exit>, C<signal> and C<timed_out> always describe the original
command, including a diff exit code indicating differences. A filter error
makes the tool result an error and adds C<filter_error>, while retaining that
command status. The command may already have completed; do not rerun it
merely because its output filter failed.

=head2 Version control

C<vcs-diff> and C<vcs-status> take a required C<backend>: C<git>, C<svn>,
C<cvs> or C<hg>, a workdir, an optional C<cwd> (default C<.>), and an optional
C<files> array. C<cwd> selects a directory within the workdir; it does not
change the granted mount boundary. Files are relative to C<cwd> and must
stay within the selected workdir. The worker checks its resolved directory
after chdir and before starting the client, rejecting symlink escapes.
Backend selection is explicit. With no files, the command examines the
working copy at C<cwd>. Files must be inside the selected workdir;
they are passed as literal filenames, not options, revisions or shell code.
Git diff shows unstaged changes; SVN, CVS and Mercurial diff compare the
working copy with its base. No commit, update, fetch or push is performed.

These tools have separate C<vcs-diff> and C<vcs-status> permission actions.
They request C<workdir-read> for the selected working copy. CVS additionally
requests C<ssh-agent>, which includes network access. Git, SVN and Mercurial
perform these local operations without network or home access.
A saved approval can cover the fixed operation regardless of filenames or
output filters, for that workdir and backend, or all supported backends.
Configured VCS clients and their configuration are trusted, including any
helpers they invoke; these tools do not sandbox the client's own semantics.
The ordinary sandbox grants still bound its host access. In unsandboxed
mode, the normal full-authority approval requirement applies.

Clients are resolved at startup. C<executable.git>, C<executable.svn>,
C<executable.cvs> and C<executable.hg> in trusted program/instance settings
can override them. A missing client produces a tool error for that backend.

=head1 REQUIREMENTS

Bubblewrap is required by default, but can be disabled explicitly.
Perl 5.8.4 or later must run in the worker environment at startup (except
for C<--exec-cmd>). GNU find, grep, patch and sed are optional; tools
requiring an unavailable utility are omitted. Use C<tools/list> to see
which tools are available.

=head1 CONNECTIONS

HTTP is the default and does not print its URL on startup. C<--print-url>
prints the credential-bearing URL; the same token works in a bearer header.
C<--stdio> or
C<PERL_ANYEVENT_MCP_MULTIBROKER_PROTOCOL=stdio> selects stdin/stdout.
See L<AnyEvent::MCP/GLOBAL CONFIGURATION> for named HTTP settings.

=head1 OPTIONS

Common options, including C<--name>, C<--bind>, C<--token>,
C<--stdio>, C<--connect>, C<--print-url>, C<--auto-allow> and
C<--auto-deny>, are documented in
L<AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Simple/mcp_getoptions>.
The default service-instance name is C<default>; C<--name> also selects
the filesystem permission-rule file described below.
Unlike ordinary startup, C<--print-url> does not require a workdir.
C<--tool-help> prints the tools available with the selected configuration
and installed utilities, including argument types and descriptions, then
exits without starting the MCP listener. Supply the usual WORKDIR arguments.

For example, C<--auto-allow '*,inspect,workdir-read'> together with
C<--auto-allow '*,read,workdir-read'> approves only those action classes
within workdir-read authority. C<--auto-allow '*,*,workdir-write'> approves
any action needing at most workdir-write authority (including read and no grants).

=over

=item --exec-cmd SANDBOX-GRANT,...

Run a command with the named sandbox grants. No MCP server or permission rules
are used; the current directory is the workdir. With C<--sandbox=none> the
command runs directly with normal host-user privileges.
Defaults to C<$SHELL> or C</bin/sh> if no command is supplied.
The C<--> separator before the command is optional.

=item --ask $method

Selects permission prompting: C<helper> (default), C<tty>, C<yad>, C<mcp>, or C<auto>.
C<tty> and C<yad> force the corresponding built-in interface without fallback.
Overrides C<PERL_ANYEVENT_MCP_PERMISSION_ASK>, which can configure the
default for all servers using C<AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Permissions>.
The default keeps approval local. C<mcp> delegates to client form elicitation;
C<auto> uses elicitation when advertised and otherwise the local helper.

=item --sandbox MODE

Select C<bwrap> (default), C<auto>, or C<none>. C<bwrap> requires a usable
bubblewrap executable. C<auto> uses bubblewrap if found, otherwise runs
unsandboxed; it does not fall back if an existing executable fails.
C<none> deliberately runs workers with full host-user privileges.

In unsandboxed mode every MCP worker (including read-only tools) requires
approval for the actual maximum execution authority: world-write, network,
shared-tmp and host-proc. Narrower approvals do not authorize execution;
the server does not pretend that workdir mounts restrict direct processes.
Startup warns prominently when there is no sandbox.

=item --bwrap PATH

Selects the bubblewrap executable. Defaults to C<bwrap> in C<PATH>.

=back

Rules reside in C<$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/anyevent-mcp/rules/fs-NAME.ini>,
or the corresponding C<$HOME/.config> path. The rules filename shares the
service-instance name but has an C<fs> namespace. The default instance uses
C<rules/fs-default.ini>. The rules path is printed on stderr when starting
HTTP; normal startup leaves stdout untouched.

=head1 TRUSTED CONFIGURATION

In C<~/.config/anyevent-mcp/global.ini> (or the corresponding
C<XDG_CONFIG_HOME> location), C<[program NAME]> supplies defaults based
on the executable basename, while C<[instance NAME]> overrides them for
C<--name NAME>. A symlinked executable therefore has its own program defaults.

   [program mcp-fs]
   mount.opt = ro:/opt
   prepend-path = /opt/bin:/opt/sbin
   mount.shared = rw:/srv/shared
   env.cvs = CVS_RSH=/my/cvs/rsh
   executable.perl = /opt/bin/perl

   [instance myproject]
   mount.opt =
   prepend-path =
   mount.extra = ro:/srv/project-tools

Automatic permission rules can also be configured:

   [program mcp-fs]
   auto-rule.10 = allow *,read,workdir-read
   auto-rule.20 = allow *,write,workdir-write
   auto-rule.30 = deny *,exec,*

These apply to every named mcp-fs instance; CLI auto-rules are evaluated first.

C<mount.SUFFIX> entries are applied in alphabetical key order after
the private sandbox mounts. The suffix is an arbitrary stable name;
an empty instance value suppresses an inherited entry. Paths must be
absolute and exist. A colon after C<ro:> or C<rw:> starts the literal
pathname; no separate quoting or escaping syntax is used.

C<env.SUFFIX> entries contain C<NAME=value> to set a literal value, or
C<NAME> to inherit that variable from the server environment if present.
Values may contain spaces and equals signs; there is no shell expansion.
Entries are applied in alphabetical key order after the default environment.
An empty instance entry suppresses the corresponding program entry.
C<SSH_AUTH_SOCK> is managed exclusively by the C<ssh-agent> grant, even
when listed in these settings. Environment settings do not expose paths;
custom executables outside the runtime/workdir mounts need configured mounts.

C<prepend-path> adds colon-separated directories before the bubblewrapped
worker's fixed C<PATH> of
C</bin:/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/local/sbin>.
Trailing colons are ignored, so both C</opt/bin> and C</opt/bin:> work.
The server's C<PATH> is not inherited. An empty instance value restores the
fixed default rather than the program value. C<--sandbox=none> continues to
use the host environment. Directories outside the normal runtime mounts
still need a configured mount to be accessible.

Configured mounts grant baseline access to every sandbox invocation,
independently of tool permission grants. With C<world-read>, only C<rw:>
exceptions are applied over the read-only host filesystem. With
C<world-write>, these configured mounts are ignored. Baseline execution
paths include C</usr>, C</bin>, system libraries and selected system
configuration, but not C</opt>. Shared host temporary directories require
C<shared-tmp>, a configured mount, or a C<world-*> grant.

C<executable.find>, C<executable.grep>, C<executable.patch>,
C<executable.perl>, C<executable.sed>, and C<executable.bwrap>
override automatic executable discovery. Explicit choices must resolve to
executable files and are trusted instead of version-probed, except for
C<executable.perl>, which must satisfy the Perl minimum version.
C<executable.perl> defaults to C<perl> in the worker's C<PATH>;
the other worker executables are discovered there too. C<--bwrap>
overrides C<executable.bwrap> and is located on the host.

Defaults also include alternatives, font configuration, local timezone,
TLS certificate stores, NSS configuration and DNS configuration where
present. Unless C<world-read> or C<world-write> is granted, a synthetic
passwd entry is supplied without exposing the host passwd or group
databases. With either world grant, the host databases remain visible.

The C<workdirs> result includes C<workdir_vcs>, a mapping from configured
workdir identities to arrays of backends with metadata markers directly
in that directory. Detection checks C<CVS>, C<.git>, C<.hg> and C<.svn>;
a Git metadata file is supported as well as a directory. Symlink markers
are reported without following them. This is a hint, not validation of a
repository. There is no recursive scan or parent search, multiple markers
are retained, and an empty array means no marker was found. Detection is
independent of the available clients in C<vcs_backends> and does not choose
the backend for a VCS command.

=head1 SANDBOX GRANTS

C<workdir-read> and C<workdir-write> expose only the selected workdir;
write implies read. C<all-workdirs-read> and C<all-workdirs-write>
expose every configured workdir. C<all-workdirs-read> combined with
C<workdir-write> mounts all workdirs read-only except the selected one,
which is writable. C<all-workdirs-write> makes all writable.
Configured workdirs are mounted using resolved absolute paths, but their
permission-rule identities are verbatim command-line arguments.
Persistent permission rules default to the selected workdir, with an explicit
option to match any configured workdir, including future additions. This
controls reuse of the rule, not the workdirs accessible in one operation.
The multibroker alias is shown in permission prompts, without changing the rule file.
Tools accept C<workdir> as a persistent positive integer ID, a numeric
string containing that ID, or an exact configured or resolved workdir path.
C<workdirs> returns C<workdirs> and C<resolved_workdirs> objects keyed by ID,
not arrays indexed by argument position. IDs are shared across server
instances in C<$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/anyevent-mcp/fs-workdir-ids.json>, or under
C<$HOME/.config> when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset. Resolved absolute paths
determine IDs; permission-rule identities remain the verbatim arguments.

The registry stores C<ID =E<gt> [workdir, last_use]> pairs, with C<last_use>
as a Unix timestamp. Starting
a server resolves its IDs once and reserves any new allocations under the
file lock. An AnyEvent timer runs immediately and every 5.25 days, rereading
the registry and overwriting those fixed assignments with fresh timestamps.
Other entries expire after seven days without refresh;
their IDs can be reused. Restart servers after manually changing the registry.
Numeric IDs therefore survive restarts and argument reordering while retained,
but are not permanent references to paths that have expired.

Non-numeric strings are matched only against configured workdirs, never
interpreted as arbitrary filesystem paths. Unknown or ambiguous selectors
fail. With only one workdir, the field may be omitted.
Overlapping workdirs have unspecified mount order and access effects;
do not rely on overlapping workdir boundaries for isolation.
C<homedir-read> and C<homedir-write> similarly control HOME.
C<world-read> and C<world-write> expose the host filesystem, with write
implying read. C<network> and C<shared-tmp> independently enable networking
and the host's C</tmp> and C</var/tmp>. C<long-running> permits exec timeouts
over 300 seconds. C<host-proc> is unavailable with bubblewrap; direct execution has host-process access.

C<ssh-agent> implies C<network>. It exposes the server's C<SSH_AUTH_SOCK>
at C</run/anyevent-mcp/ssh-agent> and sets the child's variable to that path.
It also exposes C<~/.ssh/known_hosts> read-only if present, without mounting
the rest of HOME. Private key files and SSH user configuration are not exposed.
An unset or empty C<SSH_AUTH_SOCK> exposes no socket, allowing rsh-based CVS;
a configured socket must be an absolute path naming an existing socket node.
Socket replacement requires a new invocation; an existing bind mount keeps
the old node. Agent access permits authentication, not retrieval of private keys.

=head1 LIMITATIONS

With bubblewrap, regardless of granted sandbox grants, the sandbox mounts procfs at
C</proc>. Its process entries are restricted by the PID namespace, but
kernel-wide and hardware information (including C</proc/irq> and
C</proc/bus>) remains visible. This is a limitation of bubblewrap's
C<--proc> option, which does not support C<subset=pid>.

=head1 PERMISSIONS

L<AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Permissions> applies ordered allow, deny and ask
decisions to server-defined action classes. C<inspect> groups stat/list/find;
C<read> groups file-read/read-lines/grep; C<write> groups file-write/file-patch;
C<manage> covers path-op, and C<exec> covers command execution. C<file-write>
may create files despite being in the write class; manage refers specifically
to pathname operations rather than an exclusive right to create files.
Filesystem operations have no path-based persistent subjects: their rules
match C<action>, C<sandbox-grants>, and C<workdir> when selected-workdir
authority is required. All-workdirs-only requests have no workdir identity.
Rules without C<workdir> can apply to any selected workdir if their sandbox
grants cover the request; dialog-generated selected-workdir rules are exact.
Requested paths are shown in the approval dialog for information only.
Exec additionally offers persistent
command/argv subjects. When stdin is provided, exec attaches display-only
details to the permission request: Unicode text for UTF-8 input, or a
bounded escaped-byte preview for Latin-1/Base64 input. The YAD helper offers
a separate C<View stdin...> window; the preview shows input size and
truncation. This does not change authorization: persistent exec rules match
argv, not stdin, and later runs may supply different scripts or bytes.
Conversion takes place before approval and the worker receives the exact
resulting bytes.
C<file-patch> supplies C<View patch> details with the target and supplied diff.
Base64 patches are decoded for display: valid UTF-8 is shown as Unicode,
otherwise non-printable bytes and backslashes are escaped in a byte view.
The UI chooses whether to show a separate viewer or an inline preview.
Each tool checks permissions before starting its worker. Approval
defaults to the local helper selected by C<PERL_ANYEVENT_MCP_PERMISSION_HELPER>,
which tries YAD, then a terminal, then denies once. Client elicitation is
selected with C<--ask mcp>, C<--ask auto>, or the corresponding
C<PERL_ANYEVENT_MCP_PERMISSION_ASK> value.

You can edit the rule file at any time. It is reread on permission checks,
so changes apply without restarting the server. Keep it owned by your user
and private (mode 0600). See L<AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Permissions> for the
rule syntax and ordering.

Permission retries retain earlier one-time approvals within the same call.
For one worker accessing several paths, the required sandbox-grant set combines
the authority needed for all paths and is approved once. An insufficient reduction fails before
the worker starts.
Multi-path C<stat> requires all paths inside its selected workdir.
C<path-op> can attempt cross-workdir renames after approving the sandbox
grants required for both paths. The sandboxed C<rename(2)> may fail with
C<EXDEV> across mounts; no copy/delete fallback is attempted.
C<mkpath> requires a path inside the selected workdir.
C<path-op> performs requested operations in this fixed order:
C<unlink>, C<rmdir>, C<mkpath>, C<mkdir>, C<symlink>, C<truncate>,
C<chmod>, C<rename>. C<mkpath> includes each missing component in the
combined permission check before starting the worker.
The first failure stops subsequent operations; successful earlier mutations
remain in place. There is no rollback or all-or-nothing transaction.

Exec defaults to a 30-second timeout and permits up to 300 seconds without
additional timeout authority. A larger explicit C<timeout>, up to 21600
seconds (six hours), also requires C<long-running>. This capability is added
to the requested permissions automatically. Cancellation still terminates
the worker, regardless of its allowed timeout.

The C<host-proc> grant is unavailable with bubblewrap. Runtime mounts
provide sandbox machinery. Symlinks resolve within granted mounts.
Without bubblewrap, workers operate in the host mount and process namespaces.

=head1 PATCHES

C<file-patch> uses L<AnyEvent::MCP/PATCH UTILITIES> for normalization and
validation. GNU patch runs inside the granted sandbox with fuzz zero.
Successful output replaces the original by rename after checking its
identity, size, timestamps, metadata and link count. These stat checks
do not detect every concurrent edit; they are not a filesystem transaction.

=cut

use common::sense;

use MIME::Base64 ();
use Getopt::Long ();
use Cwd ();
use File::Basename ();
use File::Spec::Unix ();
use POSIX ();
use Fcntl qw(F_SETFD);
use Scalar::Util qw(weaken);

use JSON::XS ();
use AnyEvent ();
use AnyEvent::MCP ();
use AnyEvent::MCP::Config ();
use AnyEvent::MCP::Workdirs ();
use AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Permissions ();
use AnyEvent::Handle ();
use AnyEvent::Log ();
use AnyEvent::Util ();

use AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Simple;

# resolve an executable name or path to an absolute executable file.
sub find_executable {
   my ($name) = @_;

   !ref $name and length $name and $name !~ /\0/
      or return;

   my @path = $name =~ m{/}
      ? ($name)
      : map { (length $_ ? $_ : ".") . "/$name" } split /:/, $ENV{PATH};
   my $cwd = Cwd::getcwd;

   for my $path (@path) {
      next unless -f $path and -x _;
      return $path =~ m{\A/} ? $path : "$cwd/$path";
   }

   undef
}

Getopt::Long::Configure qw(bundling no_ignore_case require_order);

my $sandbox_mode = "bwrap";
my $usage = "Usage: $0 [OPTION]... WORKDIR [WORKDIR]...\n"
   . "       $0 --exec-cmd GRANT,... [--] [COMMAND [ARG]...]\n";

my %server_options = mcp_getoptions ({
   usage => $usage,
   description => "Expose filesystem tools and command execution over MCP.\n"
      . "Operations require permission and normally run inside bubblewrap.",
   option_heading => "Filesystem options:",
   options => [
      ["--ask MODE", "Ask for permission via helper (default), tty, yad, mcp, or auto"],
      ["--sandbox MODE", "Use bwrap (default), auto, or none (unsandboxed)"],
      ["--bwrap PATH", "Path to the bubblewrap executable"],
      ["--exec-cmd GRANT,...", "Run a command directly with the named grants"],
   ],
   examples => "  $0 --stdio /home/alice/src/project\n"
      . "  $0 --name project --print-url\n"
      . "  $0 --sandbox bwrap /home/alice/src/project\n"
      . "  $0 --auto-allow '*,inspect,workdir-read' --auto-allow '*,read,workdir-read' /project\n"
      . "  $0 --auto-allow '*,*,workdir-write' /project",
},
   "sandbox=s"       => \$sandbox_mode,
   "ask=s"           => \my $ask,
   "exec-cmd=s"      => \my $exec_cmd,
   "bwrap=s"         => \my $bwrap,
);

my $name = $server_options{name};
my $stdio = delete $server_options{stdio};

die "--ask must be helper, tty, yad, mcp, or auto\n"
   if defined $ask && $ask !~ /\A(?:helper|tty|yad|mcp|auto)\z/;

(defined $exec_cmd or @ARGV)
   or die $usage;

$sandbox_mode =~ /\A(?:bwrap|auto|none)\z/
   or die "--sandbox must be bwrap, auto, or none\n";

my $program_name = AnyEvent::MCP::Config::program_name ();
my $config_file = AnyEvent::MCP::Config::_file ();
my $config = -e $config_file ? AnyEvent::MCP::Config->global : undef;
my %settings = $config ? (
   %{ $config->{section}{"program $program_name"} },
   %{ $config->{section}{"instance $name"} },
) : ();

my $sandbox_path = "/bin:/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/local/sbin";
(my $prepend = $settings{"prepend-path"} // "") =~ s/:+\z//;
$sandbox_path = "$prepend:$sandbox_path" if length $prepend;

my @mount;
for my $key (sort grep /\Amount\./, keys %settings) {
   my $value = $settings{$key};
   next unless length $value;
   my ($mode, $path) = $value =~ /\A(ro|rw):(\/.*)\z/
      or die "invalid $key mount '$value'\n";
   -e $path or die "invalid $key mount path '$path'\n";
   push @mount, [$key, $mode, $path];
}

my @environment;
for my $key (sort grep /\Aenv\./, keys %settings) {
   my $value = $settings{$key};
   next unless length $value;
   my ($name, $text) = $value =~ /\A([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z_0-9]*)(?:=(.*))?\z/s
      or die "invalid $key environment entry '$value'\n";
   push @environment, [$name, $text];
}

my ($find, $grep, $patch, $perl, $sed);
my %vcs_program;

$bwrap //= $settings{"executable.bwrap"} || "bwrap";

if ($sandbox_mode ne "none") {
   my $name = $bwrap;
   $bwrap = find_executable $name;
   die "bubblewrap executable not found: $name\n"
      if !$bwrap && $sandbox_mode eq "bwrap";
} else {
   undef $bwrap;
}

my @runtime_path = grep -e, qw(
   /usr
   /bin
   /sbin
   /lib
   /lib64
   /etc/ld.so.cache
   /etc/alternatives
   /etc/fonts
   /etc/localtime
   /etc/ssl/openssl.cnf
   /etc/ssl/certs
   /etc/pki/tls/openssl.cnf
   /etc/pki/tls/certs
   /etc/pki/ca-trust
   /etc/nsswitch.conf
   /etc/hosts
   /etc/resolv.conf
);

my (@workdir, @workdir_identity);
if (defined $exec_cmd) {
   @workdir = Cwd::abs_path ".";
   @workdir or die "cannot resolve current directory\n";
   utf8::decode $workdir[0] or die "workdir is not UTF-8\n";
   @workdir_identity = @workdir;
} else {
   for my $path (@ARGV) {
      my $workdir = Cwd::abs_path $path;
      -d $workdir or die "invalid workdir '$path'\n";
      utf8::decode $workdir or die "workdir is not UTF-8\n";
      my $identity = $path;
      utf8::decode $identity or die "workdir identity is not UTF-8\n";
      push @workdir, $workdir;
      push @workdir_identity, $identity;
   }
}

my $home = $ENV{HOME};
$home =~ m{\A/} and -d $home
   or die "HOME must name an absolute existing directory\n";
$home = Cwd::abs_path $home
   // die "cannot resolve HOME\n";
utf8::decode $home
   or die "HOME is not a valid UTF-8 path\n";

my $config_home;
unless (defined $exec_cmd) {
   $config_home = $ENV{XDG_CONFIG_HOME};
   if (length $config_home) {
      $config_home =~ m{\A/}
         or die "permission config directory must be absolute\n";
      utf8::decode $config_home
         or die "permission config directory is not UTF-8\n";
   } else {
      $config_home = "$home/.config";
   }
}

my ($workdir_ids, $workdir_refresh);

unless (defined $exec_cmd) {
   $workdir_ids = AnyEvent::MCP::Workdirs->new (
      file => "$config_home/anyevent-mcp/fs-workdir-ids.json",
      paths => \@workdir,
   );

   my $interval = .75 * $workdir_ids->{expiry};
   $workdir_refresh = AE::timer 0, $interval, sub {
      $workdir_ids->refresh;
   };
}

my %sandbox_grant = (
   "workdir-read"  => [],
   "workdir-write" => ["workdir-read"],
   "all-workdirs-read" => ["workdir-read"],
   "all-workdirs-write" => [qw(all-workdirs-read workdir-write)],
   "homedir-read"     => [],
   "homedir-write"    => ["homedir-read"],
   "world-read"    => [qw(all-workdirs-read homedir-read)],
   "world-write"   => [qw(world-read all-workdirs-write homedir-write)],
   "network"       => [],
   "ssh-agent"     => ["network"],
   "long-running"  => [],
   "shared-tmp"    => [],
   "host-proc"     => [],
);

my %sandbox_grant_description = (
   "workdir-read"  => "Read files in the selected workdir",
   "workdir-write" => "Change files in the selected workdir",
   "all-workdirs-read" => "Read files in all configured workdirs",
   "all-workdirs-write" => "Change files in all configured workdirs",
   "homedir-read"     => "Read files in your home directory",
   "homedir-write"    => "Change files in your home directory",
   "world-read"    => "Read the host filesystem",
   "world-write"   => "Change the host filesystem",
   "network"       => "Use network connections",
   "ssh-agent"     => "Use your SSH agent to authenticate, read known_hosts and use network connections",
   "long-running"  => "Run commands longer than five minutes",
   "shared-tmp"    => "Access host temporary directories",
   "host-proc"     => $bwrap ? "Host process access (unavailable with bubblewrap)"
                             : "Full access to host processes",
);

my @exec_default_sandbox_grants = ("workdir-read");

my $umask = umask;

my $permissions;
$permissions = AnyEvent::MCP::Server::Permissions->new (
   sandbox_grants             => \%sandbox_grant,
   sandbox_grant_descriptions => sub {
      my ($request) = @_;

      my %description = %sandbox_grant_description;
      if (defined $request->{selected_workdir}) {
         my $name = JSON::XS->new->utf8 (0)->allow_nonref
            ->encode ($request->{selected_workdir});
         $description{"workdir-read"} = "Read files in selected workdir $name";
         $description{"workdir-write"} = "Change files in selected workdir $name";
      }
      \%description
   },
   (defined $ask ? (ask => $ask) : ()),
   auto_rules => [@{$server_options{auto_rules} || []},
      ($config ? $config->auto_rules ($name) : ())],
   sandbox_implementation => $bwrap ? "bubblewrap" : "NONE - UNSANDBOXED (full host-user privileges)",
   actions => {
      inspect => "Inspect resource metadata and enumerate names",
      read    => "Read resource contents",
      write   => "Modify resource contents",
      manage  => "Rename, remove, and manage filesystem paths",
      exec    => "Run arbitrary commands",
      "vcs-diff"   => "Read working-copy differences using a VCS client",
      "vcs-status" => "Read working-copy status using a VCS client",
   },
   name         => $name,
   file         => "$config_home/anyevent-mcp/rules/fs-$name.ini",
) unless defined $exec_cmd;

# permissions group tools by their kind of access, not by MCP tool name.
my %tool_action = (
   stat => "inspect", list => "inspect", find => "inspect",
   "file-read" => "read", "read-lines" => "read", grep => "read",
   "file-write" => "write", "file-patch" => "write",
   "path-op" => "manage", exec => "exec",
);

# normalize an API pathname lexically against its selected workdir.
sub fs_path {
   my ($workdir, $path) = @_;

   !ref $path and length $path and $path !~ /\0/
      or die "path must be a nonempty string without NUL\n";
   $path = "$workdir/$path" unless $path =~ m{\A/};

   my @part;

   for (split m{/+}, $path) {
      next if !length or $_ eq ".";
      if ($_ eq "..") {
         pop @part;
      } else {
         push @part, $_;
      }
   }

   "/" . join "/", @part
}

# test pathname containment without resolving symlinks.
sub fs_inside {
   my ($path, $directory) = @_;

   $directory eq "/" or $path eq $directory or (index $path, "$directory/") == 0
}

# classify relative to the selected workdir, not any configured workdir.
sub fs_path_capability {
   my ($workdir, $path, $mode) = @_;

   return "workdir-$mode"
      if fs_inside $path, $workdir;
   return "all-workdirs-$mode"
      if grep { fs_inside $path, $_ } @workdir;
   return "homedir-$mode" if fs_inside $path, $home;
   return "shared-tmp" if (fs_inside $path, "/tmp") or (fs_inside $path, "/var/tmp");

   "world-$mode"
}

# persist selected-workdir identities only where the requested authority
# actually depends on that selection. Broad all-workdirs grants are unscoped.
sub fs_grant_workdir {
   my ($identity, $grants) = @_;

   my $have = $permissions->closure ($grants);
   return $identity if $have->{"workdir-write"} && !$have->{"all-workdirs-write"};
   return $identity if $have->{"workdir-read"} && !$have->{"all-workdirs-read"};
   undef
}

# permission prompts display the selected, verbatim configured workdir next
# to workdir-specific grant choices. This is information, not a rule scope.
sub fs_require_permission {
   my ($req, $identity, $request) = @_;

   $request->{selected_workdir} = $identity;
   $req->require_permission ($request)
}

# direct execution has no filesystem, network or process isolation. Request
# every grant the process effectively receives, regardless of client wishes.
my @unsandboxed_grants = qw(world-write network shared-tmp host-proc);
sub fs_effective_grants {
   return @_ if $bwrap;
   (@unsandboxed_grants, grep $_ eq "long-running", @_)
}

# one worker needs the combined sandbox authority for every path it will access.
sub fs_require_paths {
   my ($req, $workdir, $identity, $paths, $mode, $reason, $details) = @_;

   my %seen;
   $paths = [grep !$seen{$_}++, @$paths];
   my %need = map { (fs_path_capability $workdir, $_, $mode) => 1 } @$paths;
   %need = map { $_ => 1 } fs_effective_grants (keys %need);
   my @grants = sort keys %need;
   my $grant = fs_require_permission ($req, $identity, {
      tool           => $req->{tool},
      action         => $tool_action{$req->{tool}},
      workdir        => fs_grant_workdir ($identity, \@grants),
      paths          => $paths,
      sandbox_grants => \@grants,
      reason         => $reason // "$req->{tool} " . join (", ", @$paths),
      (defined $details ? (details => $details) : ()),
   });

   my $have = $permissions->closure ($grant);
   for my $capability (sort keys %need) {
      $have->{$capability}
         or die "permission reduced below $capability for $req->{tool}\n";
   }

   [sort keys %$have]
}

# test whether the granted capability set makes this pathname writable.
sub fs_writable {
   my ($workdir, $path, $have) = @_;

   $have->{"world-write"}
      or ($have->{"all-workdirs-write"} and (grep { fs_inside $path, $_ } @workdir))
      or ($have->{"workdir-write"} and fs_inside $path, $workdir)
      or ($have->{"homedir-write"} and (fs_inside $path, $home))
      or ($have->{"shared-tmp"} and ((fs_inside $path, "/tmp") or (fs_inside $path, "/var/tmp")))
}

# build the bubblewrap argv implementing the granted sandbox authority.
sub fs_sandbox {
   my ($workdir, $sandbox_grants) = @_;

   my $have = {};
   my @todo = @$sandbox_grants;
   while (@todo) {
      my $capability = pop @todo;
      exists $sandbox_grant{$capability} or die "Unknown permission capability '$capability'\n";
      next if $have->{$capability}++;
      push @todo, @{$sandbox_grant{$capability}};
   }

   unless ($bwrap) {
      unless (defined $exec_cmd) {
         $have->{$_} or die "unsandboxed execution needs $_ grant\n"
            for @unsandboxed_grants;
      }
      return ();
   }

   die "host-proc sandbox authority is unresolved and unavailable in v1\n"
      if $have->{"host-proc"};

   my @command = ($bwrap, qw(
      --die-with-parent
      --unshare-user --unshare-pid --unshare-ipc --unshare-uts
      --unshare-cgroup-try --disable-userns --cap-drop ALL
   ));
   push @command, "--new-session" unless defined $exec_cmd;

   push @command, "--unshare-net" unless $have->{network};

   push @command, $have->{"world-write"} ? "--bind-try" : "--ro-bind-try", "/", "/"
      if $have->{"world-read"};

   my @scope_mount;

   unless ($have->{"world-read"}) {
      for my $path (@runtime_path) {
         my $mount = (fs_writable $workdir, $path, $have) ? "--bind-try" : "--ro-bind-try";
         push @scope_mount, $mount, $path, $path;
      }
   }

   if ($have->{"homedir-read"}
       and (!$have->{"world-read"} or ($have->{"homedir-write"} and !$have->{"world-write"}))) {
      my $mount = (fs_writable $workdir, $home, $have) ? "--bind-try" : "--ro-bind-try";
      push @scope_mount, $mount, $home, $home;
   }

   if ($have->{"all-workdirs-read"}) {
      for my $path (@workdir) {
         my $mount = ($have->{"all-workdirs-write"}
            or ($path eq $workdir && $have->{"workdir-write"}))
               ? "--bind" : "--ro-bind";
         push @scope_mount, $mount, $path, $path;
      }
   } elsif ($have->{"workdir-read"}) {
      my $mount = $have->{"workdir-write"} ? "--bind" : "--ro-bind";
      push @scope_mount, $mount, $workdir, $workdir;
   }

   # replace special host mounts even when broad filesystem authority was asked.
   push @command, qw(--tmpfs /sys --tmpfs /run --dev /dev);
   push @command, "--proc", "/proc";

   for my $tmp (qw(/tmp /var/tmp)) {
      if ($have->{"shared-tmp"} && !$have->{"world-write"}) {
         push @command, "--bind-try", $tmp, $tmp;
      } elsif (!$have->{"world-read"}
         && !(grep $_->[2] eq $tmp, @mount)) {
         push @command, "--tmpfs", $tmp;
      }
   }

   # explicit mounts override the private baseline where bwrap/kernel permit it.
   push @command, @scope_mount;
   for my $path (qw(/etc/resolv.conf /etc/hosts)) {
      my $mount = (fs_writable $workdir, $path, $have) ? "--bind-try" : "--ro-bind-try";
      push @command, $mount, $path, $path;
   }

   # configured exceptions come after workdir/home mounts. Do not let an
   # administrator's ro exception narrow explicitly granted write authority.
   unless ($have->{"world-write"}) {
      for my $entry (@mount) {
         my ($key, $mode, $path) = @$entry;
         next if $mode eq "ro" && ($have->{"world-read"} || fs_writable $workdir, $path, $have);
         push @command, ($mode eq "rw" ? "--bind" : "--ro-bind"), $path, $path;
      }
   }

   push @command, "--file", 3, "/etc/passwd"
      unless $have->{"world-read"};

   my $agent_socket;
   if ($have->{"ssh-agent"}) {
      if (length $ENV{SSH_AUTH_SOCK}) {
         $ENV{SSH_AUTH_SOCK} =~ m{\A/} && -S $ENV{SSH_AUTH_SOCK}
            or die "SSH_AUTH_SOCK must name an absolute existing socket\n";
         my $source = $ENV{SSH_AUTH_SOCK};
         utf8::decode $source or die "SSH_AUTH_SOCK is not UTF-8\n";
         $agent_socket = "/run/anyevent-mcp/ssh-agent";
         push @command, "--ro-bind", $source, $agent_socket;
      }
      push @command, "--ro-bind-try", "$home/.ssh/known_hosts", "$home/.ssh/known_hosts"
         unless $have->{"homedir-read"} || $have->{"world-read"};
   }

   push @command, "--remount-ro", "/"
      unless $have->{"world-write"};

   push @command,
      "--clearenv",
      "--setenv", "PATH", $sandbox_path,
      "--setenv", "LC_ALL", "C.UTF-8",
      "--setenv", "HOME", $home;

   for my $name (qw(TERM COLORTERM TERMINFO TERMINFO_DIRS NO_COLOR CLICOLOR CLICOLOR_FORCE LINES COLUMNS)) {
      push @command, "--setenv", $name, $ENV{$name} if defined $ENV{$name};
   }

   for my $entry (@environment) {
      my ($name, $value) = @$entry;
      next if $name eq "SSH_AUTH_SOCK";
      $value = $ENV{$name} unless defined $value;
      push @command, "--setenv", $name, $value if defined $value;
   }
   push @command, "--setenv", "SSH_AUTH_SOCK", $agent_socket if defined $agent_socket;

   push @command,
      "--chdir", defined $exec_cmd ? $workdir : $have->{"workdir-read"} ? $workdir : "/tmp",
      "--";

   @command
}

# pass a private synthetic user entry to bubblewrap without a host passwd mount.
sub fs_passwd_fd {
   my ($read, $write) = AnyEvent::Util::portable_pipe
      or die "passwd pipe: $!\n";
   print {$write} "sandbox:x:$<:" . POSIX::getgid () . ":Sandbox:$home:/bin/sh\n"
      or die "passwd pipe: $!\n";
   close $write or die "passwd pipe: $!\n";

   if (fileno ($read) != 3) {
      POSIX::dup2 (fileno ($read), 3) == 3 or die "passwd dup2: $!\n";
      close $read;
      open $read, "<&=3" or die "passwd fd: $!\n";
   }

   defined fcntl ($read, F_SETFD, 0) or die "passwd fcntl: $!\n";
   $read
}

if (defined $exec_cmd) {
   my @sandbox_grants = length $exec_cmd ? split /,/, $exec_cmd, -1 : ();
   my @command = ((fs_sandbox $workdir[0], \@sandbox_grants),
      (@ARGV ? @ARGV : ($ENV{SHELL} || "/bin/sh")));
   my $passwd_fd = fs_passwd_fd () if $bwrap;

   exec {$command[0]} @command;
   die "exec $command[0]: $!\n";
}

# Perl is required in the worker namespace; discover the other tools there too.
my $probe = q{#perl
   use 5.008004;
   use Cwd ();

   my %test = (
      find => [[qw(--version)], qr/^find \(GNU findutils\)/m],
      grep => [[qw(--version)], qr/^grep \(GNU grep\)/m],
      patch => [[qw(--version)], qr/^GNU patch /m],
      sed => [["-n", "1p", "/dev/null"], qr/\A\z/],
   );

   sub find_executable {
      my ($name) = @_;

      return unless length $name && $name !~ /\0/;
      my @path = $name =~ m{/} ? ($name)
         : map { (length $_ ? $_ : ".") . "/$name" } split /:/, $ENV{PATH}, -1;
      for (@path) {
         next unless -f $_ && -x _;
         return m{\A/} ? $_ : Cwd::getcwd () . "/$_";
      }
      undef
   }

   while (@ARGV) {
      my ($name, $wanted, $explicit) = splice @ARGV, 0, 3;
      my $path = find_executable $wanted;
      if ($path && !$explicit && $test{$name}) {
         my ($args, $match) = @{$test{$name}};
         my $ok = open my $fh, "-|", $path, @$args;
         if ($ok) {
            my $output = do { local $/; <$fh> };
            $ok = close $fh && $output =~ $match;
         }
         undef $path unless $ok;
      }
      $path ||= "";
      print "$path\0";
   }
};

my @names = qw(perl find grep patch sed git svn cvs hg);
my $perl_name = $settings{"executable.perl"} || "perl";
my @command = ($bwrap ? (fs_sandbox $workdir[0], []) : (),
   $perl_name, "-e", $probe,
   map { ($_, $settings{"executable.$_"} || $_, length $settings{"executable.$_"} ? 1 : 0) } @names);
my $pid = open my $fh, "-|";
defined $pid or die "Perl probe fork: $!\n";

unless ($pid) {
   my $passwd_fd = fs_passwd_fd () if $bwrap;
   exec {$command[0]} @command;
   warn "exec $command[0]: $!\n";
   POSIX::_exit 126;
}

my $output = do { local $/; <$fh> };
close $fh or die "Perl '$perl_name' cannot run in the worker environment\n";
my @paths = split /\0/, $output, -1;
@paths == @names + 1 && pop @paths eq ""
   or die "invalid Perl executable probe response\n";
my %program;
@program{@names} = @paths;
$program{perl} or die "Perl interpreter not found inside the sandbox\n";
($find, $grep, $patch, $perl, $sed) = @program{qw(find grep patch perl sed)};
warn "$_: not detected or wrong version\n"
   for grep !$program{$_}, qw(find grep patch sed);
%vcs_program = map { ($_ => $program{$_}) } qw(git svn cvs hg);

warn "mcp-fs: SANDBOX NONE - workers run with full host-user privileges\n"
   unless $bwrap;

AnyEvent::detect; # proc_spawn creates child watchers after they might have died

# spawn stdin/stdout and stderr handles; proc_destroy closes them and terminates the child.
# the independent child watcher remains alive until the child is reaped.
sub proc_spawn {
   my ($program, @argv) = @_;

   my ($parent, $child) = AnyEvent::Util::portable_socketpair
      or die "socketpair: $!\n";
   my ($error, $child_error) = AnyEvent::Util::portable_pipe
      or die "pipe: $!\n";

   my $pid = fork;
   defined $pid or die "fork: $!\n";

   unless ($pid) {
      open STDIN,  "<&", $child       or POSIX::_exit 126;
      open STDOUT, ">&", $child       or POSIX::_exit 126;
      open STDERR, ">&", $child_error or POSIX::_exit 126;

      close $parent;
      close $error;
      close $child;
      close $child_error;

      my $passwd_fd = fs_passwd_fd () if $bwrap;
      AnyEvent::Util::close_all_fds_except (0, 1, 2, ($bwrap ? 3 : ()));

      exec {$program} $program, @argv;
      print STDERR "exec $program: $!\n";
      POSIX::_exit 126;
   }

   close $child;
   close $child_error;

   my $io  = AnyEvent::Handle->new (fh => $parent);
   my $err = AnyEvent::Handle->new (fh => $error);
   my $proc = { pid => $pid };

   # keep the watcher alive independently so a fatal I/O error still reaps.
   $proc->{child} = AE::child $pid, sub {
      $proc->{status} = $_[1];
      delete $proc->{pid};
      delete $proc->{child};
      (delete $proc->{done})->($proc->{status}) if $proc->{done};
   };

   $io->{proc}     = $proc;
   $io->{proc_err} = $err;

   $io
}

# signal a spawned process while its pid is still owned by us.
sub proc_kill {
   my ($proc, $signal) = @_;

   my $pid = $proc->{pid} or return;

   $signal //= "KILL";
   kill $signal => $pid
}

# abandon a process and release its streams; repeated cleanup is harmless.
sub proc_destroy {
   my ($io) = @_;

   proc_kill (delete $io->{proc}) if $io->{proc};
   (delete $io->{proc_err})->destroy if $io->{proc_err};
   $io->destroy;
}

# register the process's exit callback, or call it immediately if reaped.
sub proc_on_exit {
   my ($proc, $done) = @_;

   return $done->($proc->{status})
      if defined $proc->{status};

   $proc->{done} = $done;
}

# Run a one-shot subprocess, leaving output interpretation to the caller.
# The stream callbacks consume rbuf and may return true to terminate the child.
sub proc_run {
   my ($req, $command, $input, $timeout, $on_stdout, $on_stderr, $done) = @_;

   my $io = proc_spawn @$command;
   my $err = $io->{proc_err};
   my $proc = $io->{proc};
   my ($open, $timed_out, $failure, $timer) = (2);

   my $complete = sub {
      return if $open || !defined $proc->{status};
      my $cb = $done or return;
      undef $done;
      undef $timer;
      proc_destroy $io;
      return if $req->{finished};
      delete $req->{on_cancel};
      $cb->($proc->{status}, $timed_out, $failure);
   };

   my $stop = sub {
      undef $timer;
      delete $req->{on_cancel};
      $open = 0;
      proc_destroy $io;
      $complete->();
   };
   $req->{on_cancel} = $stop;

   for my $stream ([$io, $on_stdout], [$err, $on_stderr]) {
      my ($handle, $consume) = @$stream;
      my $closed;
      my $finish = sub {
         return if $closed++;
         --$open;
         $complete->();
      };
      my $read = sub {
         my ($handle, $eof) = @_;
         my $stop = eval { $consume->($handle, $eof) };
         if ($@) {
            $failure ||= $@;
            $stop = 1;
         }
         proc_kill $proc if $stop;
      };
      $handle->on_read (sub { $read->($_[0], 0) });
      $handle->on_eof (sub {
         $read->($_[0], 1);
         $finish->();
      });
      $handle->on_error (sub {
         return unless $_[1];
         $read->($_[0], 0);
         $failure ||= "subprocess I/O: $_[2]\n" unless $timed_out;
         proc_kill $proc;
         $finish->();
      });
   }

   my $ok = eval {
      $io->push_write ($input) if length $input;
      $io->push_shutdown;
      1
   };
   unless ($ok) {
      my $error = $@;
      proc_destroy $io;
      die $error;
   }

   $timer = AE::timer $timeout, 0, sub {
      $timed_out = 1;
      $stop->();
   };

   proc_on_exit $proc, $complete;
}

# Run a subprocess and collect bounded output.
sub fs_run {
   my ($req, $command, $input, $timeout, $byte_limit, $line_limit, $done) = @_;

   my ($truncated, $line_truncated);
   my ($stdout, $stderr) = ("", "");

   my $capture = sub {
      my ($target, $data, $lines) = @_;

      return if $lines and $line_truncated;
      return unless length $data;
      my $remaining = $byte_limit - (length $stdout) - (length $stderr);
      $truncated = 1 if length $data > $remaining;
      $$target .= substr $data, 0, $remaining
         if $remaining > 0;

      if ($lines and $line_limit and ($$target =~ tr/\n//) > $line_limit) {
         my $end = -1;
         $end = index $$target, "\n", $end + 1
            for 1 .. $line_limit;
         substr $$target, $end + 1, length $$target, "";
         $truncated = $line_truncated = 1;
         return 1
      }

      0
   };

   proc_run $req, $command, $input, $timeout,
      sub {
         my ($handle) = @_;
         my $stop = $capture->(\$stdout, $handle->{rbuf}, 1);
         $handle->{rbuf} = "";
         $stop
      },
      sub {
         my ($handle) = @_;
         $capture->(\$stderr, $handle->{rbuf}, 0);
         $handle->{rbuf} = "";
         0
      },
      sub {
         my ($status, $timed_out, $failure) = @_;
         return $done->(0, $failure) if $failure;
         $done->(1, +{
            stdout    => $stdout,
            stderr    => $stderr,
            exit      => $status >> 8,
            signal    => $status & 127,
            timed_out => $timed_out ? JSON::XS::true : JSON::XS::false,
            truncated => $truncated || $timed_out ? JSON::XS::true : JSON::XS::false,
         });
      };
}

my $perl_slave = q{#perl
   binmode STDIN;
   binmode STDOUT;
   $SIG{PIPE} = "IGNORE";
   $| = 1;

   for (;;) {
      4 == read STDIN, my $buf, 4
         or last;
      my $len = unpack "N", $buf;
      $len == read STDIN, $buf, $len
         or die "short perl frame\n";

      my @result = do {
         (my $source, local @ARGV) = unpack "(w/a*)*", $buf;
         ("+", eval $source)
      };

      @result = ("-", $@)
         if $@;

      print pack "N/a*", pack "(w/a*)*", @result
         or die "stdout: $!\n"
   }
};

sub perl_frame {
   pack "N/a*", pack "(w/a*)*", @_
}

my $perl_worker;

sub perl_eval {
   my ($req, $workdir, $sandbox_grants, $source, $args, $timeout, $done) = @_;

   my @command = ((fs_sandbox $workdir, $sandbox_grants), $perl, "-e", $perl_slave);
   my $key = pack "(w/a*)*", @command;
   my $io = $perl_worker;
   undef $perl_worker;

   if ($io && ($io->{perl_key} ne $key || !$io->{proc}{pid})) {
      proc_destroy $io;
      undef $io;
   }

   unless ($io) {
      $io = proc_spawn @command;
      $io->{rbuf_max} = (1 << 20) + 4096;
      $io->{perl_key} = $key;
      weaken (my $this = $io);

      my $idle_destroy = sub {
         my $io = $this or return;

         undef $perl_worker if $perl_worker && $perl_worker == $io;
         proc_destroy $io;
      };
      my $idle_stderr = sub { $_[0]{rbuf} = "" };

      $io->{perl_idle} = sub {
         my ($io) = @_;
         my $err = $io->{proc_err};

         $io ->on_eof   ($idle_destroy);
         $io ->on_error ($idle_destroy);
         $err->on_eof   ($idle_destroy);
         $err->on_error ($idle_destroy);
         $err->on_read  ($idle_stderr );
         $io ->on_read  ($idle_destroy);
      };

      proc_on_exit $io->{proc}, sub {
         my $io = $this or return;

         $io->{perl_on_exit}->($_[0]) if $io->{perl_on_exit};
         $idle_destroy->() if $perl_worker && $perl_worker == $io;
      };
   }

   my $err = $io->{proc_err};
   my $timer;
   my $stderr = "";

   my $finish = sub {
      my ($failure, $payload) = @_;
      my $cb = $done or return;
      undef $done;
      undef $timer;
      delete $req->{on_cancel};
      delete $io->{perl_on_exit};

      if ($failure || defined $io->{proc}{status}) {
         my $status = $io->{proc}{status};
         my $reason = $failure || "exited (exit " . ($status >> 8)
            . ", signal " . ($status & 127) . ")";
         AE::log warn => "sandbox perl worker: $reason"
            unless $failure && $failure =~ /\Asandbox perl (?:cancelled|timed out)\n\z/;
         proc_destroy $io;
         return $cb->(0, $failure || "sandbox perl exited\n")
            unless $req->{finished};
         return;
      }

      my (@response, $error);
      my $ok = eval {
         @response = unpack "(w/a*)*", $payload;
         my $status = shift @response;
         defined $status or die "missing perl response status\n";
         if ($status eq "-") {
            $error = AnyEvent::MCP::utf8dec $response[0];
         } elsif ($status ne "+") {
            die "invalid perl response status\n";
         }
         1
      };

      unless ($ok) {
         my $error = $@;
         AE::log warn => "sandbox perl worker: $error";
         proc_destroy $io;
         return $cb->(0, $error) unless $req->{finished};
         return;
      }

      # the response completes the request; the interpreter remains idle.
      proc_destroy $perl_worker if $perl_worker;
      $perl_worker = $io;
      $io->{perl_idle}->($io);
      return if $req->{finished};
      return $cb->(0, $error) if defined $error;
      $cb->(1, \@response);
   };

   $io->{perl_on_exit} = sub {
      my ($status) = @_;
      $finish->("sandbox perl failed (exit " . ($status >> 8)
         . ", signal " . ($status & 127) . "): $stderr\n");
   };

   $req->{on_cancel} = sub { $finish->("sandbox perl cancelled\n") };
   $io->on_eof (sub { $finish->("short perl response\n") });
   $io->on_error (sub { $finish->("sandbox perl stdout: $_[2]\n") if $_[1] });
   $err->on_read (sub {
      $stderr .= substr $_[0]{rbuf}, 0, 65536 - length $stderr
         if length $stderr < 65536;
      $_[0]{rbuf} = "";
   });
   $err->on_eof (sub { $finish->("sandbox perl stderr closed\n") });
   $err->on_error (sub { $finish->("sandbox perl stderr: $_[2]\n") if $_[1] });

   $timer = AE::timer $timeout, 0, sub { $finish->("sandbox perl timed out\n") };
   my $ok = eval {
      $io->push_read (packstring => "N", sub {
         my ($io, $payload) = @_;

         return $finish->("unexpected sandbox perl output\n")
            if length $io->{rbuf};
         $finish->(undef, $payload);
      });
      $io->push_write (perl_frame $source, @$args);
      1
   };
   $finish->($@) unless $ok;
}

# resolve a configured workdir by persistent ID or exact path.
sub fs_workdir {
   my ($id) = @_;

   $id = $workdir_ids->{ids}[0] if !defined $id and @workdir == 1;

   defined $id && !ref $id
      or die "select a configured workdir by ID or exact path\n";

   my $i;
   if ($id =~ /\A[0-9]+\z/) {
      ($i) = grep $workdir_ids->{ids}[$_] == $id, 0 .. $#workdir;
      defined $i or die "Unknown workdir ID '$id'\n";
   } else {
      my @match = grep { $id eq $workdir_identity[$_] || $id eq $workdir[$_] } 0 .. $#workdir;
      @match == 1 or die @match
         ? "Ambiguous configured workdir '$id'\n"
         : "Unknown configured workdir '$id'\n";
      $i = $match[0];
   }

   wantarray ? ($workdir[$i], $workdir_identity[$i]) : $workdir[$i]
}

my $workdir_schema = {
   description => "Configured workdir: persistent positive integer ID (or numeric string), or exact path from workdirs/resolved_workdirs. IDs are object keys in those listings, not argument positions. Required when multiple workdirs are configured.",
   anyOf => [
      { type => "integer", minimum => 1 },
      { type => "string", minLength => 1 },
   ],
};

sub fs_result;

# command output options affect presentation, never argv or permission scope.
my %output_schema = (
   grep => { type => "string", description => "Optional PCRE line filter for stdout only. Runs after bounded capture, without a shell." },
   max_lines => { type => "integer", minimum => 1, maximum => 100000, default => 2000,
      description => "Maximum returned stdout lines after filtering." },
   max_bytes => { type => "integer", minimum => 1, maximum => 1048576, default => 65536,
      description => "Maximum returned UTF-8 bytes per stream; stderr is never filtered." },
);

sub fs_output_options {
   my ($args) = @_;

   my %options = (max_lines => $args->{max_lines} // 2000, max_bytes => $args->{max_bytes} // 65536);
   for my $name (qw(max_lines max_bytes)) {
      $options{$name} =~ /\A[0-9]+\z/ && $options{$name} >= 1
         && $options{$name} <= $output_schema{$name}{maximum}
         or die "invalid $name\n";
   }

   if (exists $args->{grep}) {
      defined $args->{grep} && !ref $args->{grep} && $args->{grep} !~ /\0/
         && length $args->{grep} <= 65536
         or die "grep must be a string without NUL, at most 65536 characters\n";
      $grep or die "output filtering needs GNU grep\n";
      $options{grep} = AnyEvent::MCP::utf8enc $args->{grep};
   }

   \%options
}

# clipping a UTF-8 stream may leave at most three bytes of an incomplete character.
sub fs_output_text {
   my ($bytes, $cut) = @_;

   for (0 .. ($cut ? 3 : 0)) {
      my $text = $bytes;
      return $text if utf8::decode $text;
      chop $bytes;
   }

   die "command output is not UTF-8\n";
}

# preserve child status while bounding capture and, separately, returned output.
sub fs_command {
   my ($req, $workdir, $grants, $argv, $input, $timeout, $options, $cwd) = @_;

   # check the actual directory inside the worker, after resolving symlinks.
   $argv = [$perl, "-MCwd", "-e", q{
      my $workdir = shift;
      chdir shift or die "VCS chdir: $!\n";
      my $cwd = Cwd::getcwd ();
      defined $cwd && ($workdir eq "/" || $cwd eq $workdir || (index $cwd, "$workdir/") == 0)
         or die "VCS cwd must resolve within the selected workdir\n";
      exec {$ARGV[0]} @ARGV;
      die "VCS exec: $!\n";
   }, $workdir, $cwd, @$argv] if defined $cwd;

   my @command = ((fs_sandbox $workdir, $grants),
      $bwrap ? @$argv
         : ($^X, "-e", 'chdir shift or die "chdir: $!"; exec @ARGV; die "exec: $!";', $workdir, @$argv));
   my ($stdout, $stderr) = ("", "");
   my ($stdout_cut, $stderr_cut);

   my $capture = sub {
      my ($handle, $out, $cut) = @_;
      my $remaining = 1048576 - length $$out;
      $$cut = 1 if length $handle->{rbuf} > $remaining;
      $$out .= substr $handle->{rbuf}, 0, $remaining if $remaining > 0;
      $handle->{rbuf} = "";
      0
   };

   proc_run $req, \@command, $input, $timeout,
      sub { $capture->($_[0], \$stdout, \$stdout_cut) },
      sub { $capture->($_[0], \$stderr, \$stderr_cut) },
      sub {
         my ($status, $timed_out, $failure) = @_;
         return $req->error ($failure) if $failure;

         my $result = {
            exit => $status >> 8, signal => $status & 127,
            timed_out => $timed_out ? JSON::XS::true : JSON::XS::false,
            capture_truncated => $stdout_cut || $stderr_cut || $timed_out ? JSON::XS::true : JSON::XS::false,
         };

         my $finish = sub {
            my $ok = eval {
               $stdout_cut = 1 if length $stdout > $options->{max_bytes};
               $stderr_cut = 1 if length $stderr > $options->{max_bytes};
               my $out = fs_output_text (substr ($stdout, 0, $options->{max_bytes}), $stdout_cut);
               my $err = fs_output_text (substr ($stderr, 0, $options->{max_bytes}), $stderr_cut);
               my @lines = split /(?<=\n)/, $out;
               if (@lines > $options->{max_lines}) {
                  splice @lines, $options->{max_lines};
                  $out = join "", @lines;
                  $stdout_cut = 1;
               }
               @$result{qw(stdout stderr)} = ($out, $err);
               $result->{stdout_truncated} = $stdout_cut || $timed_out ? JSON::XS::true : JSON::XS::false;
               $result->{stderr_truncated} = $stderr_cut || $timed_out ? JSON::XS::true : JSON::XS::false;
               $result->{truncated} = $stdout_cut || $stderr_cut || $timed_out ? JSON::XS::true : JSON::XS::false;
               1
            };
            return $req->error ($@) unless $ok;
            $req->reply (fs_result $result, exists $result->{filter_error});
         };

         return $finish->() unless exists $options->{grep};

         {
            my $ok = eval { $stdout = AnyEvent::MCP::utf8enc (fs_output_text $stdout, $stdout_cut); 1 };
            return $req->error ($@) unless $ok;
         }

         # regex execution stays in a timed worker, outside the broker's event loop.
         my @filter = ((fs_sandbox $workdir, $grants), $grep, "-aP", "--", $options->{grep});
         fs_run $req, \@filter, $stdout, 5, 1048576, 0, sub {
            my ($success, $filtered) = @_;
            if (!$success || $filtered->{exit} > 1 || $filtered->{signal} || $filtered->{timed_out}) {
               my $error = $success ? $filtered->{stderr} : $filtered;
               $error = eval { fs_output_text (substr ($error, 0, 4096), 1) } || "no diagnostic";
               $result->{filter_error} = "stdout filter failed: $error";
               $stdout = "";
               $stdout_cut = 1;
            } else {
               $stdout = $filtered->{stdout};
               $stdout_cut ||= $filtered->{truncated};
            }
            $finish->();
         };
      };
}

# wrap raw text as an MCP text result.
sub fs_text_result {
   my ($text, $failed) = @_;

   my $response = {
      content => [{
         type => "text",
         text => $text,
      }],
   };

   $response->{isError} = JSON::XS::true if $failed;

   $response
}

# wrap a Perl value as an MCP text result.
sub fs_result {
   my ($result, $failed) = @_;

   my $response = fs_text_result (JSON::XS->new->encode ($result), $failed);
   $response->{structuredContent} = $result;

   $response
}

mcp_tool workdirs =>
   "List configured workdirs, VCS metadata markers at each workdir (without recursion or parent search), available VCS clients, sandbox grants, trusted mounts and runtime paths.",
   {
      type                 => "object",
      properties           => {},
      additionalProperties => JSON::XS::false,
   },
   sub {
      my ($req, $args) = @_;

      AE::log debug => "fs workdirs";

      # report local metadata markers, not an inferred repository for the tree.
      my %marker = (cvs => "CVS", git => ".git", hg => ".hg", svn => ".svn");
      my %workdir_vcs = map {
         my $i = $_;
         ($workdir_identity[$i] => [grep {
            lstat "$workdir[$i]/$marker{$_}" && (-d _ || -l _ || ($_ eq "git" && -f _))
         } sort keys %marker])
      } 0 .. $#workdir;

      $req->reply (fs_result +{
         workdirs                   => { map { $workdir_ids->{ids}[$_] => $workdir_identity[$_] } reverse 0 .. $#workdir },
         resolved_workdirs          => { map { $workdir_ids->{ids}[$_] => $workdir[$_] } 0 .. $#workdir },
         workdir_vcs                => \%workdir_vcs,
         vcs_backends               => [sort grep defined $vcs_program{$_}, keys %vcs_program],
         umask                      => (sprintf "%04o", $umask),
         runtime_paths              => \@runtime_path,
         mounts                     => \@mount,
         sandbox_grants             => \%sandbox_grant,
         exec_default_sandbox_grants => \@exec_default_sandbox_grants,
         unavailable_sandbox_grants  => ["host-proc"],
      });
   }
;

# escape a pathname for compact line-oriented text output.
sub escape_path {
   my ($text) = @_;

   $text =~ s/\\/\\\\/g;
   $text =~ s/\n/\\n/g;
   $text
}

sub format_iec_number {
   my ($number) = @_;

   for my $suffix ("", qw(k M G T P)) {
      return "$number$suffix" if $number < 1024 or $suffix eq "P";
      $number = int (($number + 512) / 1024);
   }
}

sub fs_find_relative {
   my ($base, $path) = @_;

   return "" if $path eq $base;
   substr $path, length ($base) + ($base eq "/" ? 0 : 1)
}

sub format_path_listing {
   my ($base, $record, $truncated) = @_;

   my (@line, $directory);

   for my $record (sort { $a->[2] cmp $b->[2] } @$record) {
      my ($type, $size, $full_path) = @$record;
      my $relative = fs_find_relative $base, $full_path;

      if ($type eq "d") {
         push @line, "/" . (escape_path $relative);
         $directory = $relative;
         next;
      }

      my ($dir, $name);

      if (!length $relative) {
         ($name) = $full_path =~ m{([^/]*)\z};
      } else {
         ($dir, $name) = $relative =~ m{\A(?:(.*)/)?([^/]*)\z};
         $dir //= "";

         if (!defined $directory or $directory ne $dir) {
            push @line, "/" . (escape_path $dir);
            $directory = $dir;
         }
      }

      $name = escape_path $name;
      push @line, $type eq "f"
         ? (join "\t", $type, (format_iec_number $size), $name)
         : (join "\t", $type, $name);
   }

   push @line, "!truncated" if $truncated;

   join "", map "$_\n", @line
}

sub fs_list_find {
   my ($req, $args, $recurse) = @_;

   my $tool = $recurse ? "find" : "list";

   my ($workdir, $identity) = fs_workdir $args->{workdir};
   my $path = fs_path $workdir, $args->{path} // ".";
   my $limit = $args->{limit} // 200;
   $limit <= 1000
      or die "invalid limit\n";

   my $granted = fs_require_paths ($req, $workdir, $identity, [$path], "read",
      $args->{reason} // "$tool $path");

   if ($recurse) {
      AE::log debug => join " ", "fs", $tool, $path, (substr $args->{name}, 0, 40);
   } else {
      AE::log debug => "fs $tool $path";
   }

   my $find_path = AnyEvent::MCP::utf8enc $path;
   my @command = ((fs_sandbox $workdir, $granted), $find, $find_path);

   if ($recurse) {
      my $needle = AnyEvent::MCP::utf8enc $args->{name};
      $needle =~ s/([\\*?\[])/\\$1/g;
      push @command, "!", "-type", "d", "-name", "*$needle*";
   } else {
      push @command, "-maxdepth", "1";
   }

   push @command, "-printf", q{%y\0%s\0%p\0};

   my (@record, $seen, $truncated, $failure);
   my $stderr = "";

   proc_run $req, \@command, "", 30,
      sub {
         my ($handle, $eof) = @_;

         while (!$failure and !$truncated
                and $handle->{rbuf} =~ s/\A([^\0]*)\0([^\0]*)\0([^\0]*)\0//s) {
            my ($type, $size, $raw_path) = ($1, $2, $3);
            my $full_path = eval { AnyEvent::MCP::utf8dec $raw_path };

            if ($@) {
               $failure = "$tool returned a non-UTF-8 pathname\n";
               last;
            }

            if (++$seen > $limit) {
               $truncated = 1;
               last;
            }

            push @record, [$type, $size, $full_path];
         }

         $failure ||= "incomplete $tool output\n"
            if $eof && length $handle->{rbuf} && !$truncated;
         $handle->{rbuf} = "" if $failure or $truncated;
         $failure || $truncated
      },
      sub {
         my ($handle) = @_;
         my $remaining = 65536 - length $stderr;
         $stderr .= substr $handle->{rbuf}, 0, $remaining if $remaining > 0;
         $handle->{rbuf} = "";
         0
      },
      sub {
         my ($status, $timed_out, $io_failure) = @_;
         return if $req->{finished};

         my $result;
         my $ok = eval {
            die "$tool timed out\n" if $timed_out;
            die $failure || $io_failure if $failure || $io_failure;

            if (($status >> 8 or $status & 127) and !$truncated) {
               my $error = eval { AnyEvent::MCP::utf8dec $stderr };
               $error = "non-UTF-8 error output" if $@;
               die "$tool failed (exit " . ($status >> 8) . ", signal " . ($status & 127) . "): $error\n";
            }

            $result = fs_text_result (format_path_listing $path, \@record, $truncated);
            1
         };

         if ($ok) {
            $req->reply ($result);
         } else {
            $req->error ($@);
         }
      };
}

$find && mcp_tool list =>
   "List immediate filesystem entries without following symlinks. "
   . "Compact text output uses /relative-directory headers for directories, f<TAB>approx-size<TAB>name "
   . "for regular files, TYPE<TAB>name for l/p/s/b/c/? nodes, "
   . "with compact size suffixes k/M/G/T/P using IEC powers of 1024, "
   . "and !truncated when the result limit was exceeded. Backslash and newline are escaped in names.",
   {
      type                 => "object",
      properties           => {
         path   => {
            type        => "string",
            description => "Directory to list; defaults to the selected workdir.",
         },
         limit  => {
            type    => "integer",
            minimum => 1,
            maximum => 1000,
            default => 200,
         },
         workdir => $workdir_schema,
         reason => {
            type        => "string",
            description => "Reason for requesting permission.",
         },
      },
      additionalProperties => JSON::XS::false,
   },
   sub {
      fs_list_find $_[0], $_[1], 0
   }
;

